186
00:13:42,250 --> 00:13:44,210
So why do we useState?

187
00:13:48,010 --> 00:13:53,690
It's a hook that
is optimized to run

188
00:13:53,690 --> 00:14:03,690
in React components in this
UI any time that a state

189
00:14:03,690 --> 00:14:07,950
variable or a variable that's
tracked with useState updates,

190
00:14:07,950 --> 00:14:12,130
anytime that updates the
browser is going to re-render

191
00:14:12,130 --> 00:14:18,570
this screen, or at least it'll
re-render this component and any

192
00:14:18,570 --> 00:14:23,590
of its children that depend
on the variable that updated.

193
00:14:23,590 --> 00:14:26,970
So in this case,
this return statement

194
00:14:26,970 --> 00:14:28,310
has this variable in it.

195
00:14:28,310 --> 00:14:36,370
So we're going to re-render
this whole component.

196
00:14:36,370 --> 00:14:40,010
But let's say we had this
component nested inside

197
00:14:40,010 --> 00:14:43,560
of another screen or
some other component,

198
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:46,180
we might not want to
re-render that entire screen,

199
00:14:46,180 --> 00:14:48,280
especially if our app
is complex and there

200
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:56,040
are some components that are
costly in terms of performance

201
00:14:56,040 --> 00:14:56,700
to render.

202
00:14:56,700 --> 00:14:58,760
We only want to
re-render the portion

203
00:14:58,760 --> 00:15:02,720
of that screen that needs to.

204
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:04,380
That's why we use useState.

205
00:15:04,380 --> 00:15:07,720
This will optimize the
rerendering of our app

206
00:15:07,720 --> 00:15:09,340
whenever our data changes.

