decrement that counter.

252
00:19:18,140 --> 00:19:24,600
And then if we want to
write some text in here.

253
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:31,040
And we can type that in.

254
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:35,840
And we're adding these text
items to this list down here.

255
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:49,100
All right.

256
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:51,980
So let's look at--

257
00:19:51,980 --> 00:19:55,120
the image toggle is the same.

258
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:58,560
We have IsImageVisible
variable there

259
00:19:58,560 --> 00:20:02,220
that's tracking whether or
not this image is rendered.

260
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:08,360
The counter section has
text which says counter.

261
00:20:08,360 --> 00:20:12,480
And then this is
with these brackets,

262
00:20:12,480 --> 00:20:14,600
that's how you get some
code logic in there.

263
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:16,620
So it normally inside
of text, whatever

264
00:20:16,620 --> 00:20:19,580
is in between the
two text tags, this

265
00:20:19,580 --> 00:20:23,460
is your text that's going to
be rendered in there directly.

266
00:20:23,460 --> 00:20:27,220
But we have a dynamic
piece of text in this case.

267
00:20:27,220 --> 00:20:29,880
So we're going to wrap that
in these curly brackets.

268
00:20:29,880 --> 00:20:33,170
And then just put the
variable right in there.

269
00:20:33,170 --> 00:20:39,450
And in this case, the
variable is the counter value

270
00:20:39,450 --> 00:20:44,210
that we have set up
here with useState.

271
00:20:44,210 --> 00:20:48,790
So that's how it's updating
that counter text dynamically.

272
00:20:48,790 --> 00:20:53,130
And then we've got some onPress
props here for our buttons.

273
00:20:53,130 --> 00:20:54,890
And that just set--

274
00:20:54,890 --> 00:20:56,770
they call set counter.

275
00:20:56,770 --> 00:21:00,650
And we're getting the previous
counter value plus 1 or minus 1.

276
00:21:00,650 --> 00:21:01,810
Simple enough.

277
00:21:01,810 --> 00:21:05,370
And then we have a text input.

278
00:21:05,370 --> 00:21:09,670
Text input, again, this is just
imported from React Native.

279
00:21:09,670 --> 00:21:11,670
All of these are imported
from React Native.

280
00:21:11,670 --> 00:21:16,910
We don't have any sort of
special UI library involved yet.

281
00:21:20,690 --> 00:21:26,890
Then we've got this text input,
takes a style placeholder text.

282
00:21:26,890 --> 00:21:31,750
And then the value
is this text value.

283
00:21:31,750 --> 00:21:38,570
So this is actually one that we
are also tracking with useState.

284
00:21:38,570 --> 00:21:43,650
So anytime that text changes,
we have access to whatever

285
00:21:43,650 --> 00:21:45,810
the text is right here.

286
00:21:45,810 --> 00:21:49,390
And then we're setting the text.

287
00:21:49,390 --> 00:21:52,170
We're passing this to
the onChange Handler

288
00:21:52,170 --> 00:21:53,510
to set the text.

289
00:21:53,510 --> 00:22:00,890
So the value of our text input
here is tracked by useState.

290
00:22:00,890 --> 00:22:06,130
And so the text input
can track that for us.

291
00:22:06,130 --> 00:22:07,310
This is also useful.

292
00:22:07,310 --> 00:22:13,350
Like if we have a custom
form validation function,

293
00:22:13,350 --> 00:22:17,190
we can define that function.

294
00:22:17,190 --> 00:22:20,330
And we have access
to that text variable

295
00:22:20,330 --> 00:22:22,570
anywhere that we need it.

296
00:22:22,570 --> 00:22:24,290
And then we have this button.

297
00:22:24,290 --> 00:22:28,580
Button has an onPress
handler, addItemToList.

298
00:22:28,580 --> 00:22:33,860
And that's calling text.trim.

299
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:39,180
And that trims the
white space like spaces,

300
00:22:39,180 --> 00:22:43,500
or enter new line
characters from the text.

301
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:50,040
And then it's calling setItems,
which is the setter in useState

302
00:22:50,040 --> 00:22:53,160
to set the items.

303
00:22:53,160 --> 00:22:56,640
Or let's see, it's
setting the array of items

304
00:22:56,640 --> 00:23:01,920
to a new array with all of
the previous items in it,

305
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:07,720
as well as text.trim.

306
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:08,780
Oh yeah.

307
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:13,300
So it's calling it here
to see if it's valid text,

308
00:23:13,300 --> 00:23:15,880
and then it's actually trimming
it and adding it to the list

309
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:16,420
there.

310
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:23,220
And then it's calling
setText to an empty string.

311
00:23:23,220 --> 00:23:29,000
So it's resetting the
text in that text input.

312
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:32,760
All of that's handled
with useState.

313
00:23:32,760 --> 00:23:36,360
That's an example of how
we can get some dynamic UI

314
00:23:36,360 --> 00:23:37,720
elements here.

315
00:23:37,720 --> 00:23:41,480
The benefit is useState
is highly optimized

316
00:23:41,480 --> 00:23:44,600
to work in React apps.

317
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:47,440
The downside is,
let's say you wanted

318
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:51,120
to know what some of
the data from this page

319
00:23:51,120 --> 00:23:54,720
is over on this page,
you don't have access

320
00:23:54,720 --> 00:23:57,880
to any of those useState
variables in here.

321
00:23:57,880 --> 00:24:00,820
That is what redux is for.

322
00:24:00,820 --> 00:24:06,240
We will go into using redux
a little bit more next week.

323
00:24:06,240 --> 00:24:08,280
We'll start into that next week.

324
00:24:08,280 --> 00:24:12,320
Redux is-- it functioned
very much the same way

325
00:24:12,320 --> 00:24:14,500
as useState does.

326
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:19,000
You have your variables and
a way to set those variables.

327
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:22,440
But the redux
variables are available

328
00:24:22,440 --> 00:24:25,400
throughout our entire app.

329
00:24:25,400 --> 00:24:30,390
So useState is for managing
state within a single component.

330
00:24:30,390 --> 00:24:36,110
We will use redux to manage
state throughout the entire Expo

331
00:24:36,110 --> 00:24:37,310
app.

332
00:24:37,310 --> 00:24:39,170
We'll be getting
into that next week.

333
00:24:41,790 --> 00:24:48,510
So we have this code that's
dynamically powering our UI here

334
00:24:48,510 --> 00:24:50,670
on our page.

335
00:24:50,670 --> 00:24:55,390
I'm going to toggle the dark
off hold for flash bang.

336
00:24:55,390 --> 00:24:59,910
So this is what
the UI looks like.

337
00:24:59,910 --> 00:25:03,470
But I think we can do
a little bit better.

338
00:25:03,470 --> 00:25:07,870
And a good way to make
sure that across our apps

339
00:25:07,870 --> 00:25:11,470
and across our pages, that
we have a consistent UI

340
00:25:11,470 --> 00:25:16,750
is to use some sort of design
system or component library

341
00:25:16,750 --> 00:25:19,670
that we use throughout our apps.

342
00:25:19,670 --> 00:25:24,950
Some people, some organizations
will make their own.

343
00:25:24,950 --> 00:25:27,190
They'll have in there--

344
00:25:27,190 --> 00:25:30,670
they'll have a components
folder full of a bunch of text

345
00:25:30,670 --> 00:25:34,470
components, let's say they
have a view or a button

346
00:25:34,470 --> 00:25:37,850
or their text that is
styled a certain way.

347
00:25:37,850 --> 00:25:41,350
They want it to be consistent
throughout the app.

348
00:25:41,350 --> 00:25:44,830
People may make their own.

349
00:25:44,830 --> 00:25:49,870
Where I work at JB Hunt, we have
our own in-house design system.

350
00:25:49,870 --> 00:25:54,430
So our brand has a
unique look and feel.

351
00:25:54,430 --> 00:25:56,870
And all of those
UI components are

352
00:25:56,870 --> 00:26:00,130
used throughout the
different JB Hunt apps.

353
00:26:00,130 --> 00:26:03,150
For example, I work
on the carrier team.

354
00:26:03,150 --> 00:26:06,150
Carrier is an external
user-facing app,

355
00:26:06,150 --> 00:26:11,550
but we also have internal facing
apps like the drive app or match

356
00:26:11,550 --> 00:26:14,290
or there's the shipper app.

357
00:26:14,290 --> 00:26:18,270
That's another
externally facing app.

358
00:26:18,270 --> 00:26:22,070
All of them use-- or
at least most of them--

359
00:26:22,070 --> 00:26:26,300
all use the same aim
component library.

360
00:26:26,300 --> 00:26:28,820
Some of the apps, like
Carriers, are a little bit older

361
00:26:28,820 --> 00:26:32,260
and hasn't fully adopted
the new component library

362
00:26:32,260 --> 00:26:33,840
that we've built in-house.

363
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:36,220
But for the most
part, our apps are

364
00:26:36,220 --> 00:26:39,320
expected to use a common
component library.

365
00:26:39,320 --> 00:26:42,220
So we have a consistent
look and feel.

366
00:26:42,220 --> 00:26:45,740
You could make your own
component library here

367
00:26:45,740 --> 00:26:52,860
for your app in this class or
that usually takes a long time.

368
00:26:52,860 --> 00:26:59,060
So one that I recommend that
you can use here in this class

369
00:26:59,060 --> 00:27:00,980
is react-native-paper.

370
00:27:04,860 --> 00:27:12,540
This has been my favorite
component library for a minute,

371
00:27:12,540 --> 00:27:16,420
mainly because it works well
with React Native cross-platform

372
00:27:16,420 --> 00:27:16,920
apps.

373
00:27:16,920 --> 00:27:19,560
And it also it supports
web and mobile.

374
00:27:19,560 --> 00:27:21,900
It's kind of hard
to find a component

375
00:27:21,900 --> 00:27:28,100
library that will support
web and mobile for React.

376
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:30,300
But it does a pretty good job.

377
00:27:30,300 --> 00:27:32,820
So let's go ahead and grab--

378
00:27:32,820 --> 00:27:34,800
we haven't made
any edits to this.

379
00:27:34,800 --> 00:27:41,860
So it should still be
fresh in our GPT's memory.

